OK.

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:48.120
So coming back to
the last lecture,

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:53.150
we discussed what's the energy
carried by electromagnetic wave

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:54.980
that's Poynting vector.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:59.720
And then we took the
plane wave solution

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:02.690
of electromagnetic
wave in a medium,

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:05.180
and we calculate
the Poynting vector.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:09.920
And from that, we learned
that the energy, the Poynting

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:16.370
vector, decays exponentially,
as is determined

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:18.680
by the absorption
coefficient, which

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:22.520
is a 4 pi kappa over lambda.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:25.100
And recall, for
optical waves, lambda

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:27.650
is 10 to the minus fifth, right?

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:31.520
So essentially, kappa could
be a very small number, 10

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:34.640
to the minus 7, and it's
very still very important,

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:38.810
because if you do 4 pi,
and 10 to the minus 7

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:41.430
divided by 10 to the
minus 6, you get 1.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:47.660
And that means it's still 1--

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.740
OK, it depends on the
length, of course.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:52.850
It's the exponential alpha x.

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:56.090
So this x is--

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:59.570
when delta is inverse--

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:04.220
x equals delta, that equals
inverse of the alpha,

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:06.440
this exponential is really 1.

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:10.130
So that delta is the
penetration depth,

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:14.630
as I said, when the
light intensity decays

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:17.430
to exponential minus 1.

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:21.510
So that's the EM wave
propagation inside the medium.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:23.790
That's a [INAUDIBLE] we said.

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:29.480
And then we started looking at
the reflection and refraction

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:32.460
of an EM wave at an interface.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:37.350
And for that, we have to be
careful of the polarization.

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:42.000
Say for electric field, if
it's pointing in any direction,

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.010
we can decompose it into
a TM wave and a TE wave.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:49.380
And the plane of
incidence depends

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:51.870
on-- defined based
on the wave vector

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:54.210
of the incident and the norm.

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:57.360
So if the TM is a
transverse magnetic,

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.640
that's an electric field
that is in the plane.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.980
That's why the parallel.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:05.640
This p means parallel
and simple parallel.

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:08.375
And TE is a transverse
electric wave.

00:05:08.375 --> 00:05:09.750
And so the electric
field that is

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:13.290
perpendicular to the plane of
the incidence, perpendicular.

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:16.830
And s-- s is probably German
for the perpendicular,

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:18.180
if anybody knows German.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:27.210
Then we have-- based on the
boundary condition derived

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:30.330
from Maxwell's equation,
we can eventually

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:34.740
write down what's the
reflection coefficient.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:37.320
there's no reflectance,
because it's

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:40.020
a ratio of the reflected
electrical field.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:41.790
It's the field ratio.

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:45.220
Reflection coefficient and
transmission coefficient.

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:48.510
So I'm not going to
repeat this formula.

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:52.980
But essentially, you can
calculate the reflectance

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:56.100
based on reflection coefficient
and the transmission

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:56.950
coefficient.

00:05:56.950 --> 00:06:00.060
You can calculate
reflectance and transmittance

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:04.570
for any angle of
incidence as long

00:06:04.570 --> 00:06:09.340
as you know the refractive index
of both sides of the medium.

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:12.550
And this is, of course,
assuming perfect interface.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:15.310
Remember, we have not added--

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:19.180
I made a comment of this
wave vector conservation.

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:21.580
So this is a
specular reflection.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:24.160
And that's what the
Snell law tells us.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:29.440
So the angle of incidence
equals angle of Reflection

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:33.070
And I say I like to always--

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:37.430
if you want to show your
knowledge, and very useful,

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:40.570
this is always, I
say for my student,

00:06:40.570 --> 00:06:44.510
I say a skill that you should
develop over the graduate years

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:46.940
if you haven't developed at
your undergraduate years,

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:49.030
is when you walk
out, you solve over

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:51.805
90% of the problem
in five minutes.

00:06:51.805 --> 00:06:55.510
And so they might say it's a
back-of-the-envelope skill,

00:06:55.510 --> 00:06:59.590
because so somebody gave
me a material, I'd say, OK,

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:03.190
that's a refractive index range,
and that's what I know roughly

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:05.080
what's the reflection.

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:09.160
So this is a perpendicular
incidence formula.

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:11.860
And that's-- you don't need to
remember the angular dependent.

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:14.200
But this is the one
that I keep in my mind

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:19.150
when I do my back-of-envelope
given any surface.

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:25.190
And so we used glass and
silicon as an example.

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:32.170
So you can see the typical
reflections of these materials.

00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:35.480
And I stopped there.

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:38.470
I want to do more discussion.

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:46.240
One, we see if we look at
the Snell law, the incidence,

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:53.260
the wave vector conservation,
so we've got n1 sine theta 1--

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:56.080
theta i-- that's the
angle of incidence--

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:00.460
equals n2 sine theta t.

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:02.740
This is the transmittance.

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:12.430
So we have-- this is the light
comes in, and typically air,

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:15.610
which is a vacuum--

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:19.060
pretty much a vacuum
depending on the wavelength.

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:23.020
And the refractive index is 1.

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:27.460
And then inside the material,
typically refractive index

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:29.260
is larger than 1.

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:32.919
And so your angle is bending--

00:08:32.919 --> 00:08:37.750
the refractive angle
theta t becomes smaller.

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:40.750
So this is Snell's law.

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:44.200
But you could have
a opposite case,

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:49.840
where the light is traveling
inside the medium and goes out.

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:50.590
t.

00:08:50.590 --> 00:08:52.150
After it goes to
the first surface,

00:08:52.150 --> 00:08:54.550
it may go, say,
inside the medium.

00:08:54.550 --> 00:08:58.450
And so in that case, if n1--

00:08:58.450 --> 00:09:05.510
so if n1 is larger
than n2, and you

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:10.940
can see my reflective
angle, theta t,

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:14.210
is larger than theta i.

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:17.450
So this is my theta t.

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:19.610
It's larger than theta i.

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:22.130
And then eventually, you
come to the situation

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:24.500
where the theta
t goes 90 degree.

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:26.750
So you ask, what
happens after that.

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:36.420
So if theta t equals 90
degrees, you get a sine theta i.

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:41.820
And we say this is a critical
angle equals to n2 over n1.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:44.550
And you can see this
ratio is less than 1.

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:49.860
So this angle-- above this
angle, where theta i is larger

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:57.420
than theta i critical and
you go to check the, say,

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:00.150
reflectance and
transmittance formula,

00:10:00.150 --> 00:10:03.150
and you'll find that r equals 1.

00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:06.830
So the light is
totally reflected.

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:10.330
Reflectance is 100%.

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:12.400
So r equals 1.

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:15.580
And that's the total reflection.

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:24.750
So total-- sometimes the
total internal reflection--

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:32.990
So this is-- internal
means, for example,

00:10:32.990 --> 00:10:36.370
if you go from inside
the glass into air,

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:40.680
the light is confined
in the glass.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:43.310
And so it's interesting.

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:45.650
You can put in the
typical number.

00:10:45.650 --> 00:10:52.400
If this is air, it's going
from glass to air, air is 1,

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:53.720
glass is 1.5.

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:55.740
So 1 by 1.5 arcsine.

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:03.890
I think in that case,
I get 42 degrees.

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:06.800
So that's actually
a very large angle.

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:08.960
If you have glass, and--

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:14.510
what it means, if this is 42,
so any light with an incidence

00:11:14.510 --> 00:11:17.530
angle larger than 42 degrees
will not escape the glass.

00:11:20.110 --> 00:11:21.160
OK.

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:27.090
So this is the principle
of optical fibers.

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:31.480
So optical fiber
has an inner core.

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:35.340
And then say it
has an outer core.

00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:39.850
And this outer core has a
smaller refractive index.

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:43.770
So during the glass
deposition, pulling they

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:48.310
change the composition
all of the outer layer

00:11:48.310 --> 00:11:51.800
to make the outer layer
smaller refractive index,

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:53.800
so that when your
light comes in,

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:59.020
it will keep bending inside.

00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:02.490
So when you use
the optical fiber,

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:05.190
you don't want to
bend it really sharp,

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:07.410
because that angle
of incidence will

00:12:07.410 --> 00:12:09.780
be above the critical angle,
the light will leak out.

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:17.540
And in fact, yesterday I was
in Baltimore having lunch with

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:20.630
somebody who was-- he
said he talked to--

00:12:20.630 --> 00:12:24.110
you know this optical
fiber was awarded a Nobel

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:27.020
Prize a few years back.

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:29.560
So it's a very simple idea.

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:33.090
And he said that he--
because I was having lunch

00:12:33.090 --> 00:12:35.850
with somebody from Corning,
so we're talking glass.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:42.840
And this guy was saying that
he asked the Corning inventor

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:45.030
about the idea at the time.

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:46.860
It was proposed--
he was proposing

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:52.320
using this for the
communication application,

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:54.770
and how people reacted.

00:12:54.770 --> 00:12:57.590
And people were just
saying, that's crazy.

00:12:57.590 --> 00:12:58.880
You're thinking about glass.

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:01.780
Because before that,
everybody used communication

00:13:01.780 --> 00:13:03.950
in electrical wires.

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:08.500
Going from wire,
electrical wire, to glass,

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:14.260
you can see some of the
even very simple idea

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:18.340
actually gets very
hard to be accepted.

00:13:18.340 --> 00:13:22.750
This internal reflection is
really simple physics-wise,

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:24.460
right?

00:13:24.460 --> 00:13:27.460
But there are a
lot of neat ideas

00:13:27.460 --> 00:13:30.250
and a lot of applications.

00:13:30.250 --> 00:13:33.370
And if you are engineer,
the simpler is better.

00:13:33.370 --> 00:13:36.670
And I can share a few.

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:41.200
So if you think about
I said glass is 1.5.

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:45.230
If you do silicon,
silicon is 3.5.

00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:49.870
1 over 3.5 arcsine gives you 16.

00:13:49.870 --> 00:13:52.740
Here I have 16.6 degrees.

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:58.050
So which means
sitcom, all the light

00:13:58.050 --> 00:14:04.960
about 16 degree coming to
the surface doesn't go out.

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:08.770
So this is where people
use this idea to do

00:14:08.770 --> 00:14:10.720
light trapping in silicon.

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:13.390
We said silicon is an
indirect semiconductor.

00:14:13.390 --> 00:14:15.980
It's not good absorption.

00:14:15.980 --> 00:14:19.010
And so if somehow if
you think about this,

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:20.630
OK, I have surface here.

00:14:20.630 --> 00:14:22.730
I've got a roughness coming in.

00:14:22.730 --> 00:14:24.350
The light comes in here.

00:14:24.350 --> 00:14:28.740
So that's reflected
over angle into here.

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:30.870
And then the outer
surface is-- if it's

00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:34.080
totally internal reflected,
it will come back.

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:39.180
So you have the longer
distance and bounce more times.

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:42.810
And that's how you can
reduce the thickness

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:48.090
and yet still achieve higher
absorption, light trapping.

00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:51.840
And this is a still very
active field of research.

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:53.420
This is geometric optics.

00:14:53.420 --> 00:14:55.560
We're just talking about
trajectory of light,

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:57.840
didn't consider the phase.

00:14:57.840 --> 00:15:01.440
But how to actually achieve
maximum light trapping

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:04.970
with the smallest material
is-- we have, for example,

00:15:04.970 --> 00:15:09.090
a DOE project trying to
make thinnest solar cell.

00:15:09.090 --> 00:15:11.040
But they still have--

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:14.910
silicon based, but still
achieve high efficiency.

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:18.122
And let me give you another--

00:15:18.122 --> 00:15:19.830
there are many, many
examples using this.

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:28.470
I remember the peak cell for
the optics, the photonics,

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:30.690
if you're, say, a few--

00:15:30.690 --> 00:15:33.000
probably around 10 years
ago, there was a big bubble,

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:34.830
like housing bubble--

00:15:34.830 --> 00:15:35.880
not as large.

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:38.070
But the stock went
through the roof.

00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:42.270
A lot of optics communication
companies got very rich.

00:15:42.270 --> 00:15:46.770
And one, because
that's the internet,

00:15:46.770 --> 00:15:50.190
and after that
it's the internet.

00:15:50.190 --> 00:15:54.840
So one of the challenges is how
you switch light directions.

00:15:57.460 --> 00:16:02.800
And there was this neat
idea doing MEMS switching,

00:16:02.800 --> 00:16:04.570
micro switching.

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:11.850
So this idea was to say,
basically you have a channel,

00:16:11.850 --> 00:16:13.740
and you have light comes in.

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:17.640
And it's typically totally
internally reflected,

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:20.670
so it doesn't go through.

00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:25.410
And then next time
you push a liquid

00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:28.050
that has the same
refractive index as a glass

00:16:28.050 --> 00:16:34.120
or very close, in that
case, you an index match.

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:36.480
It doesn't have a total
internal reflection anymore,

00:16:36.480 --> 00:16:38.790
so it just goes through.

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:40.740
It's a very simple idea.

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:42.600
And I think that was from HP.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:48.270
I don't think they were
successful commercializing it,

00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:49.270
the study.

00:16:49.270 --> 00:16:54.610
And in fact, all the
lasers use this effect.

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:57.330
So not all but the most lasers.

00:16:57.330 --> 00:17:00.600
So the solid state
laser, what it has

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:06.329
is a quantum well, very
thin gallium arsenide.

00:17:06.329 --> 00:17:09.329
And this is--
quantum well is where

00:17:09.329 --> 00:17:17.319
electrons and holes recombine
and give off a photon.

00:17:17.319 --> 00:17:20.079
And the thickness of the
world is about 100 angstrom

00:17:20.079 --> 00:17:21.710
or thinner.

00:17:21.710 --> 00:17:24.800
But the light wavelength
is much larger.

00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:26.750
So what it has is another--

00:17:26.750 --> 00:17:29.510
there is another
cladding optical layer.

00:17:29.510 --> 00:17:32.090
So this is the charge.

00:17:32.090 --> 00:17:35.450
And the electromagnetic
wave goes this way.

00:17:35.450 --> 00:17:39.530
And the refractive index of the
surrounding layer is smaller.

00:17:39.530 --> 00:17:45.250
So the light is forced to bounce
along the layer direction.

00:17:45.250 --> 00:17:50.280
So this effect is the widely
used in many, many devices.

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:55.770
Very simple, and it's a
total internal reflection.

00:17:55.770 --> 00:17:56.270
OK.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:03.250
But what's-- there is even
something more interesting.

00:18:03.250 --> 00:18:07.840
And that is, you can see
that when theta i is larger

00:18:07.840 --> 00:18:14.400
than theta, say, above critical
angle, the reflectance is 1.

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:19.440
But t, the transmission
coefficient is not 1--

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:21.190
0.

00:18:21.190 --> 00:18:27.190
And in fact, you can go to check
the transmission coefficient

00:18:27.190 --> 00:18:33.990
for we derive is 2
n1 and cosine theta i

00:18:33.990 --> 00:18:41.800
and n2 cosine theta
i plus n1 sine--

00:18:41.800 --> 00:18:43.720
cosine theta t.

00:18:46.360 --> 00:18:47.680
OK.

00:18:47.680 --> 00:18:51.490
Cosine theta t, I didn't
say mathematically.

00:18:51.490 --> 00:18:58.120
I didn't say it's 1--
it's 0, because we have--

00:18:58.120 --> 00:19:01.780
theta i is larger than
critical of angle of incidence.

00:19:01.780 --> 00:19:06.460
That means I can still use
Snell's law, sine theta i.

00:19:06.460 --> 00:19:09.010
Mathematically, that's a
solution of Maxwell equation.

00:19:09.010 --> 00:19:12.980
Didn't say Maxwell
equation didn't exclude it.

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:16.100
So theta i, if you
go to go to do this,

00:19:16.100 --> 00:19:19.400
you'll see it's a larger
than 1, sine theta 1.

00:19:22.040 --> 00:19:29.270
And-- no, sine theta t,
the angle of transmittance.

00:19:29.270 --> 00:19:30.140
It's imaginary.

00:19:30.140 --> 00:19:33.350
You go sine-- that's
an imaginary number.

00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:41.180
And cosine theta t equals 1
minus sine theta t squared.

00:19:41.180 --> 00:19:45.270
And this is larger than 1, so
this is an imaginary number.

00:19:45.270 --> 00:19:49.180
Right so let's say
it's i times d.

00:19:49.180 --> 00:19:52.000
So I substitute this
one into the-- back

00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:57.120
into the transmission
coefficient, ti's now 0.

00:20:00.590 --> 00:20:03.425
And I look at the field
on the other side.

00:20:06.280 --> 00:20:11.360
So if you look at the
transmitted field, of course,

00:20:11.360 --> 00:20:13.920
it's proportional to the
transmission coefficient.

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:19.470
And the incident field, maybe
the anger is not quite right.

00:20:19.470 --> 00:20:22.350
So let me write it just
in the proportional.

00:20:22.350 --> 00:20:27.090
And then I have my exponential
factor, the phase factor

00:20:27.090 --> 00:20:35.810
minus i omega t minus
n2x sine theta t

00:20:35.810 --> 00:20:43.720
plus n2z cosine theta t over c0.

00:20:46.570 --> 00:20:48.880
So that's my-- this
is not zero this

00:20:48.880 --> 00:20:53.220
is the incident cry my electric
field on the other side

00:20:53.220 --> 00:20:53.940
is not 0.

00:20:58.445 --> 00:21:03.200
And what happens is cosine
theta t is imaginary.

00:21:03.200 --> 00:21:04.930
When cosine theta
t is imaginary,

00:21:04.930 --> 00:21:06.980
I have an imaginary
number here, I

00:21:06.980 --> 00:21:10.480
have another imaginary
number here, it

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:13.750
gives me a real number.

00:21:13.750 --> 00:21:17.230
And that real number is
the exponential decay.

00:21:17.230 --> 00:21:20.940
So my electric field
on the other side

00:21:20.940 --> 00:21:24.300
is exponential decay function.

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:26.320
And this is the evanescent wave.

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:31.560
So when my anger n1 is n2,
and the angle of incidence

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:35.550
a larger than critical
angle, the electrical field

00:21:35.550 --> 00:21:41.330
on the other side, exponential
decay from the surface.

00:21:41.330 --> 00:21:46.140
And the real part,
the x component,

00:21:46.140 --> 00:21:50.000
if you look at n2 sine theta
t equals n1 sine theta i,

00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:53.030
so that's actually real.

00:21:53.030 --> 00:21:56.330
So this wave, what
you have is a wave

00:21:56.330 --> 00:22:00.710
propagating in the x-direction
on the surface and decays

00:22:00.710 --> 00:22:02.360
exponentially on the other side.

00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:06.720
And this is the evanescent wave.

00:22:16.880 --> 00:22:18.440
OK.

00:22:18.440 --> 00:22:21.320
And but you say, how
is that possible?

00:22:21.320 --> 00:22:24.842
You have a field, the
transmission is not--

00:22:24.842 --> 00:22:27.500
is 0.

00:22:27.500 --> 00:22:36.090
So what it happens is you go to
calculate, although the t is,

00:22:36.090 --> 00:22:41.780
itself is not 0, but tau,
the transmitted energy, is 0.

00:22:41.780 --> 00:22:43.730
The average Poynting
vector is 0.

00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:47.070
So the field comes
in, goes out back.

00:22:47.070 --> 00:22:51.410
So average speaking, there's no
net energy going from one side

00:22:51.410 --> 00:22:53.540
to the other.

00:22:53.540 --> 00:22:56.360
That's what the evanescent wave.

00:22:56.360 --> 00:23:00.920
And recall in the case
of discussing electrons,

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:04.130
that's our situation, right?

00:23:04.130 --> 00:23:09.450
We, say, OK your energy of
the electron comes in, goes--

00:23:09.450 --> 00:23:14.270
so if the energy is less
than the barrier height,

00:23:14.270 --> 00:23:16.400
your wave function on
this side is not 0.

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:23.040
But you calculated
the material flux,

00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:26.450
the particle flux is
0 on the other side.

00:23:26.450 --> 00:23:29.610
That's an evanescent
field for material wave.

00:23:29.610 --> 00:23:35.080
So all waves have
this characteristic.

00:23:35.080 --> 00:23:37.720
And how fast the decay?

00:23:37.720 --> 00:23:41.380
You go to check,
here is essentially,

00:23:41.380 --> 00:23:45.400
if you say omega-- or
C0 and the theta divide,

00:23:45.400 --> 00:23:47.350
it's of the order of wavelength.

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:51.640
So that decay is of the
order of wavelength.

00:23:51.640 --> 00:23:53.920
And then we said that,
OK, for material wave,

00:23:53.920 --> 00:23:57.190
the wavelength maybe
is a few angstroms.

00:23:57.190 --> 00:24:01.510
Last time, we said people use
that to do the IBM interview

00:24:01.510 --> 00:24:05.020
example I told you, [INAUDIBLE]
got a scanning tunneling

00:24:05.020 --> 00:24:06.130
microscope.

00:24:06.130 --> 00:24:08.570
And so you use the
evanescent wave for material

00:24:08.570 --> 00:24:09.940
with electrons.

00:24:09.940 --> 00:24:12.940
Here, you get the evanescent
wave on the other side.

00:24:12.940 --> 00:24:16.000
And there are a lot of,
say, different applications.

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:19.860
And I'll discuss more later on.

00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:21.180
OK.

00:24:21.180 --> 00:24:28.800
So this is the Snell's
law and evanescent wave.

00:24:28.800 --> 00:24:31.950
And let me also make
one more comment,

00:24:31.950 --> 00:24:35.920
because here, all our
derivation is assuming n1,

00:24:35.920 --> 00:24:38.340
n2, they are real.

00:24:38.340 --> 00:24:48.650
And if n2 is
complex, and go-- you

00:24:48.650 --> 00:24:51.920
go to check your equation,
derivation process, no problem.

00:24:51.920 --> 00:24:55.400
Everything still is applicable.

00:24:55.400 --> 00:25:00.410
It's a mathematical solution
of the Maxwell equation.

00:25:00.410 --> 00:25:06.230
So what it means is when you
look at your Snell's law,

00:25:06.230 --> 00:25:10.375
n1 sine theta 1, the i-- let
me assume n1 is real first.

00:25:13.320 --> 00:25:16.500
And you go-- you
get n2 is complex,

00:25:16.500 --> 00:25:18.210
so sine theta t is complex now.

00:25:20.990 --> 00:25:22.910
So sine theta t is complex.

00:25:26.730 --> 00:25:28.980
It's a plus bi.

00:25:28.980 --> 00:25:34.680
And cosine theta t, you
do operation, c plus di.

00:25:34.680 --> 00:25:37.300
So both are complex.

00:25:37.300 --> 00:25:41.730
And then so the same formula,
you do your complex number

00:25:41.730 --> 00:25:42.450
operation.

00:25:45.640 --> 00:25:52.660
But when both are complex, you
go to look at your wave here.

00:25:52.660 --> 00:25:54.940
Sine theta t is complex.

00:25:54.940 --> 00:25:58.300
Cosine theta t-- OK, in
fact, here it will be real.

00:25:58.300 --> 00:26:04.310
But what you will find out
is that this wave, inside,

00:26:04.310 --> 00:26:06.340
when it propagates
into a medium,

00:26:06.340 --> 00:26:10.610
the constant
amplitude is actually,

00:26:10.610 --> 00:26:12.935
in the z-direction, the
constant amplitude surface.

00:26:15.760 --> 00:26:19.080
The amplitude,
that's when z, each z

00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:23.560
equals constant that field
is a constant amplitude.

00:26:23.560 --> 00:26:27.980
But constant phase is going
in the other direction.

00:26:27.980 --> 00:26:30.130
So the constant phase
and constant amplitude

00:26:30.130 --> 00:26:32.050
actually do not coincide.

00:26:32.050 --> 00:26:35.790
And this is an
inhomogeneous wave.

00:26:35.790 --> 00:26:39.920
But say all the formula
we gave still applicable.

00:26:39.920 --> 00:26:45.430
So there's no problem as long
as you do your complex number

00:26:45.430 --> 00:26:47.770
operation consistently.

00:26:47.770 --> 00:26:51.530
And I remember that in old
days, I only know Fortran,

00:26:51.530 --> 00:26:54.580
so you have to be very
careful to declare

00:26:54.580 --> 00:26:56.260
your complex numbers.

00:26:56.260 --> 00:26:58.090
Otherwise, you
run into problems.

00:27:02.960 --> 00:27:04.490
One more.

00:27:04.490 --> 00:27:06.665
What if our n1 is also complex?

00:27:09.560 --> 00:27:11.240
n1 is also complex.

00:27:15.410 --> 00:27:17.300
And this is a point that
probably most people

00:27:17.300 --> 00:27:19.060
don't know.

00:27:19.060 --> 00:27:20.720
Everything is still applicable.

00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:22.030
So most people know that.

00:27:25.170 --> 00:27:28.710
But if you do your
operation, you'll

00:27:28.710 --> 00:27:34.110
find that R plus tau
do not equal to 1.

00:27:39.840 --> 00:27:43.800
And at the surface,
there's an absorption.

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:50.030
So I-- actually, I
remember what I found this

00:27:50.030 --> 00:27:52.280
out when I was a graduate
student doing all the math.

00:27:52.280 --> 00:27:54.950
And I said, what's
happening there?

00:27:54.950 --> 00:27:58.940
I went-- I went to ask
my professor and said,

00:27:58.940 --> 00:28:00.980
that's strange.

00:28:00.980 --> 00:28:02.690
So I went to check
a lot of books.

00:28:02.690 --> 00:28:05.130
I thought that I
could write a paper.

00:28:05.130 --> 00:28:07.160
It turns out I was
only 100 years late.

00:28:10.220 --> 00:28:12.830
It was Max Born,
in fact, that they

00:28:12.830 --> 00:28:14.990
discussed all those issues.

00:28:14.990 --> 00:28:15.860
Max Born is--

00:28:18.650 --> 00:28:22.040
Born got the--

00:28:22.040 --> 00:28:24.560
I forgot whether he got
a Nobel prize or not.

00:28:24.560 --> 00:28:28.790
But it's all in there,
discussed before.

00:28:28.790 --> 00:28:29.870
OK.

00:28:29.870 --> 00:28:35.630
So this is all the discussion
that I didn't finish.

00:28:35.630 --> 00:28:41.240
And so we now know at least
how to calculate the reflection

00:28:41.240 --> 00:28:44.420
of electron at the interface.

00:28:44.420 --> 00:28:45.950
We gave a normal incidence.

00:28:45.950 --> 00:28:49.060
I said that you should
be able to do any angle.

00:28:49.060 --> 00:28:53.780
We did the electromagnetic
wave at the interface.

00:28:53.780 --> 00:28:57.470
And the next one
is acoustic wave.

00:28:57.470 --> 00:29:01.190
So all these waves are
similar, but the equations

00:29:01.190 --> 00:29:02.030
are different.

00:29:02.030 --> 00:29:04.760
One is the
Schroedinger equation.

00:29:04.760 --> 00:29:06.870
Here is the Maxwell equation.

00:29:06.870 --> 00:29:10.820
So let me briefly talk
about acoustic wave.

00:29:17.760 --> 00:29:20.790
This is my last, because
I go from simpler

00:29:20.790 --> 00:29:21.880
to more complicated.

00:29:21.880 --> 00:29:24.640
And material wave, in fact,
the Schroedinger equation

00:29:24.640 --> 00:29:27.670
there is a scalar.

00:29:27.670 --> 00:29:32.540
Maxwell equation is a
transverse wave, two components.

00:29:32.540 --> 00:29:35.330
And you learned the
phonon's acoustic wave,

00:29:35.330 --> 00:29:37.310
has three components--
longitudinal

00:29:37.310 --> 00:29:39.920
with two transverse waves.

00:29:39.920 --> 00:29:44.010
So your equation will
be a lot more complex.

00:29:44.010 --> 00:29:58.260
And in the continuum
limit, in continuum limit,

00:29:58.260 --> 00:30:01.170
you can write a set of
differential equation

00:30:01.170 --> 00:30:03.900
for acoustic wave
propagation in a medium.

00:30:03.900 --> 00:30:07.670
And the way to derive that
equation is starting from F

00:30:07.670 --> 00:30:08.460
equals ma.

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:15.130
There are not many equations.

00:30:15.130 --> 00:30:18.970
And so the F equals ma.

00:30:18.970 --> 00:30:22.150
And then we need to say,
inside the material,

00:30:22.150 --> 00:30:27.070
how I can convert this into
the atomic displacement?

00:30:27.070 --> 00:30:31.030
Because acoustic wave,
essentially the atoms--

00:30:31.030 --> 00:30:35.710
the material displacement
from equilibrium position.

00:30:35.710 --> 00:30:43.660
So if you say, OK, displacement
is the r minus r0--

00:30:43.660 --> 00:30:45.560
that's the
equilibrium position--

00:30:45.560 --> 00:30:52.082
then a is just the derivative,
second-order derivative.

00:30:55.860 --> 00:30:59.990
So now you can try to see how
you can relate both sides so

00:30:59.990 --> 00:31:02.120
that you get one equation--

00:31:02.120 --> 00:31:07.400
one question for displacement or
velocity of the material wave.

00:31:07.400 --> 00:31:12.110
And the way you do it
is think about the force

00:31:12.110 --> 00:31:15.140
is related to stress
in the material.

00:31:17.960 --> 00:31:23.750
And stress is related to strain.

00:31:23.750 --> 00:31:27.110
Strain is related
to displacement.

00:31:27.110 --> 00:31:29.590
So if you write all
those relations,

00:31:29.590 --> 00:31:34.050
you immediately get a wave
equation for the displacement.

00:31:34.050 --> 00:31:37.680
And that's the
acoustic equation.

00:31:37.680 --> 00:31:41.250
OK, just a few more on that.

00:31:41.250 --> 00:31:49.470
And what you have here is the
strain, typical way of writing

00:31:49.470 --> 00:31:49.970
strain--

00:31:49.970 --> 00:31:52.300
I'm not going to write
all the equations.

00:31:52.300 --> 00:31:58.710
So strain is a
tensor and is defined

00:31:58.710 --> 00:32:05.250
as dui dxj plus duj dxi.

00:32:09.410 --> 00:32:11.390
That's the strain.

00:32:11.390 --> 00:32:20.060
And stress tensor-- so we have--

00:32:20.060 --> 00:32:25.160
what's the Hooke's
law in the material,

00:32:25.160 --> 00:32:30.950
in the complicated form,
the most general form,

00:32:30.950 --> 00:32:32.520
is really a tensor.

00:32:32.520 --> 00:32:38.220
So the force-- the stress
is related to the strain.

00:32:40.990 --> 00:32:44.080
And it's related
to the strain, s.

00:32:44.080 --> 00:32:47.560
So these two hats means
second-order tensor.

00:32:50.210 --> 00:32:59.060
And that coefficient is the
stiffness-- material stiffness,

00:32:59.060 --> 00:33:01.100
elastic stiffness, constant.

00:33:01.100 --> 00:33:06.190
And this one is
something times tensor.

00:33:06.190 --> 00:33:08.680
It's actually-- to get
a second-order tensor

00:33:08.680 --> 00:33:10.720
from a second-order
tensor multiplication,

00:33:10.720 --> 00:33:13.850
this should be a
fourth order tensor.

00:33:13.850 --> 00:33:18.290
So this is really
fourth-order tensor.

00:33:18.290 --> 00:33:21.500
And each order is a
three-component vector

00:33:21.500 --> 00:33:22.640
with three components.

00:33:22.640 --> 00:33:25.160
Second-order tensor
has nine components.

00:33:25.160 --> 00:33:29.650
Fourth order tensor
is 81 components.

00:33:29.650 --> 00:33:32.580
So anybody doing
solid mechanics,

00:33:32.580 --> 00:33:35.700
you must be very good at math.

00:33:35.700 --> 00:33:39.000
That's a lot of
tensor operation.

00:33:39.000 --> 00:33:42.510
And in the case of--

00:33:42.510 --> 00:33:45.110
in, fact that you
can do symmetry.

00:33:45.110 --> 00:33:47.790
You can simplify 81 to 36.

00:33:47.790 --> 00:33:51.390
And if you get a isotopic
material, you get only two so.

00:33:51.390 --> 00:33:55.770
That's what the different
assumptions mean.

00:33:55.770 --> 00:33:58.800
But essentially, so
you have displacement,

00:33:58.800 --> 00:34:01.935
you have your stress,
and your force.

00:34:04.590 --> 00:34:06.730
Generally, if you
have a surface,

00:34:06.730 --> 00:34:11.100
you have a norm, you have
stress tensor here, sigma.

00:34:11.100 --> 00:34:13.560
You have n is norm.

00:34:13.560 --> 00:34:17.520
Then they say, is
the stress tensor

00:34:17.520 --> 00:34:19.949
the normal times
a stress tensor.

00:34:19.949 --> 00:34:25.790
That gives you the force
acting on that surface.

00:34:25.790 --> 00:34:31.090
So essentially, here
those relations can me--

00:34:31.090 --> 00:34:35.929
if I substitute everything
back into the F equals ma,

00:34:35.929 --> 00:34:40.460
I should get acoustic wave
equation in the material.

00:34:40.460 --> 00:34:45.750
But except here, if you have
to look at this, I should--

00:34:45.750 --> 00:34:47.960
I know this one,
I get the time--

00:34:47.960 --> 00:34:50.840
second-order time derivative.

00:34:50.840 --> 00:34:54.380
On the left-hand side, I should
get the space second-order

00:34:54.380 --> 00:34:57.470
derivative to get the
wave equation, right?

00:34:57.470 --> 00:35:00.170
So the way I just
wrote the F equation

00:35:00.170 --> 00:35:04.310
sigma will give a sigma
equals proportional to s.

00:35:04.310 --> 00:35:07.180
That's only first
order in space.

00:35:07.180 --> 00:35:10.630
That's not second
order in space yet.

00:35:10.630 --> 00:35:14.830
And the reason is, you have
to do your finite element

00:35:14.830 --> 00:35:15.700
analysis.

00:35:15.700 --> 00:35:17.770
So you take a
differentiable volume.

00:35:17.770 --> 00:35:20.110
You have force difference.

00:35:20.110 --> 00:35:21.820
That's the left force.

00:35:21.820 --> 00:35:24.460
That difference will give
you the space second-order

00:35:24.460 --> 00:35:27.100
derivative.

00:35:27.100 --> 00:35:29.430
And so you get the
second-order derivative

00:35:29.430 --> 00:35:32.440
on the left-hand side,
second-order time derivatives

00:35:32.440 --> 00:35:33.800
in the right-hand side.

00:35:33.800 --> 00:35:37.570
You get the wave equation
that's acoustic wave equation.

00:35:37.570 --> 00:35:40.720
You can see if I write it down,
it will be very, very long.

00:35:45.970 --> 00:35:50.020
So this is-- in
general, you can have

00:35:50.020 --> 00:35:52.480
the equation for acoustic wave.

00:35:52.480 --> 00:35:56.890
And you can also calculate
there is also acoustic

00:35:56.890 --> 00:35:59.410
Poynting vector.

00:35:59.410 --> 00:36:00.175
That's the energy.

00:36:07.670 --> 00:36:10.520
The time average, the
acoustic Poynting vector--

00:36:10.520 --> 00:36:12.050
this is not the strain--

00:36:12.050 --> 00:36:17.760
Poynting vector is 1/2
real part of velocity,

00:36:17.760 --> 00:36:20.000
which is the
first-order derivative,

00:36:20.000 --> 00:36:22.950
and times this stress tensor.

00:36:22.950 --> 00:36:28.630
So this is like the
force times the velocity

00:36:28.630 --> 00:36:29.750
gives you the work.

00:36:29.750 --> 00:36:35.360
That's the acoustic
energy flux there.

00:36:35.360 --> 00:36:36.200
OK.

00:36:36.200 --> 00:36:40.490
And now, what happens
for acoustic wave

00:36:40.490 --> 00:36:42.050
coming towards the interface?

00:36:46.540 --> 00:36:51.140
And when we have
electromagnetic wave,

00:36:51.140 --> 00:36:56.010
we have, of course,
reflection and refraction.

00:36:56.010 --> 00:36:59.240
And that's the same we have.

00:36:59.240 --> 00:37:02.000
But it depends on
whether the incoming wave

00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:05.240
is a longitudinal wave,
is a transverse wave.

00:37:05.240 --> 00:37:08.010
And if it's a transverse
wave, which direction

00:37:08.010 --> 00:37:12.760
I project the
plane of incidence.

00:37:12.760 --> 00:37:19.500
And so if the displacement
is in the plane of incidence,

00:37:19.500 --> 00:37:21.930
that's an SV wave.

00:37:21.930 --> 00:37:25.680
And here, longitudinal
wave, this is L wave.

00:37:28.830 --> 00:37:34.580
And in most general case, what
happens is you do your-- again,

00:37:34.580 --> 00:37:36.840
at the interface you
have boundary condition,

00:37:36.840 --> 00:37:38.180
and you can derive--

00:37:38.180 --> 00:37:42.070
and it turns out,
in general case SV

00:37:42.070 --> 00:37:47.160
wave or a longitudinal
wave can excite three wave.

00:37:47.160 --> 00:37:50.190
You can have also
longitudinal wave.

00:37:50.190 --> 00:37:52.910
You can have SV wave and SH--

00:37:52.910 --> 00:37:57.140
SH is the other
polarization perpendicular

00:37:57.140 --> 00:37:59.540
to the plane of incidence.

00:37:59.540 --> 00:38:02.630
So that's the complication
for acoustic waves--

00:38:02.630 --> 00:38:09.810
you have mode conversion from
one mode, one polarization

00:38:09.810 --> 00:38:11.530
to other polarization.

00:38:11.530 --> 00:38:15.780
And because of this, you can
also have transmitted also

00:38:15.780 --> 00:38:17.476
have three polarizations.

00:38:20.200 --> 00:38:25.330
And if it's isotropic medium,
you only have two branches.

00:38:25.330 --> 00:38:27.130
So this will be
degenerate, and you

00:38:27.130 --> 00:38:34.650
can have L waves and
the transverse wave.

00:38:34.650 --> 00:38:38.600
So this is the general case.

00:38:38.600 --> 00:38:41.640
And the formula, in fact--

00:38:41.640 --> 00:38:43.970
there was a really
good book when--

00:38:43.970 --> 00:38:46.070
that's a book by Auld.

00:38:46.070 --> 00:38:50.330
I would suggest for anybody
interested in those, because it

00:38:50.330 --> 00:38:55.460
actually treats Maxwell equation
and acoustic wave equations

00:38:55.460 --> 00:38:57.390
side by side.

00:38:57.390 --> 00:38:59.950
And that gives a
very good learning.

00:38:59.950 --> 00:39:02.700
And when I read acoustic
wave, so that's the--

00:39:02.700 --> 00:39:08.330
I know Maxwell question, that's
how I learned acoustic wave.

00:39:08.330 --> 00:39:09.730
OK.

00:39:09.730 --> 00:39:15.010
And now if you go to do--

00:39:15.010 --> 00:39:17.920
I'm not going to give you
any angular relation the.

00:39:17.920 --> 00:39:19.600
Reflectance of acoustic wave.

00:39:22.590 --> 00:39:27.120
At the lower incidence, it's
very similar to the reflectance

00:39:27.120 --> 00:39:29.760
of optical wave at
lower incidence,

00:39:29.760 --> 00:39:32.950
and same as the electron
waves at lower incidence.

00:39:32.950 --> 00:39:41.520
So reflectance is z1 minus
z2 and z1 plus z2 squared.

00:39:41.520 --> 00:39:47.070
Well, this is-- z is density
times velocity square root.

00:39:47.070 --> 00:39:48.990
And this is acoustic impedance.

00:40:00.790 --> 00:40:05.110
So it's density times
velocity, speed of sound.

00:40:05.110 --> 00:40:07.210
You can roughly think that.

00:40:07.210 --> 00:40:12.180
And again, it's the difference
in acoustic impedance

00:40:12.180 --> 00:40:15.410
that gives the reflection
of acoustic waves.

00:40:17.980 --> 00:40:20.830
And you can write
the Snell's law.

00:40:20.830 --> 00:40:23.290
And for example, you're--

00:40:23.290 --> 00:40:26.620
the problem is for
Snell's law, this

00:40:26.620 --> 00:40:31.690
is the vi, velocity
of the incident wave.

00:40:31.690 --> 00:40:36.420
But even on the same side,
you can have reflection

00:40:36.420 --> 00:40:39.640
into other angle due
to mode conversion.

00:40:39.640 --> 00:40:44.020
The lateral momentum,
the kx, has to conserve.

00:40:44.020 --> 00:40:45.820
That's the Snell's law.

00:40:45.820 --> 00:40:52.240
And so what you have is
even for the same side--

00:40:52.240 --> 00:40:57.820
let's say reflected also
for longitudinal, let's say.

00:40:57.820 --> 00:41:00.980
Then you have to see
the longitudinal wave,

00:41:00.980 --> 00:41:03.350
there's the angle
there, angular relation,

00:41:03.350 --> 00:41:06.185
that essentially tells you the
lateral momentum conservation.

00:41:06.185 --> 00:41:08.930
And then for the other
side, you have similar.

00:41:08.930 --> 00:41:20.260
If it's transmitted,
wave t transmitted,

00:41:20.260 --> 00:41:24.970
on the, let's say, reflective
side, transmitted side.

00:41:24.970 --> 00:41:27.720
So those are the
corresponding Snell's laws

00:41:27.720 --> 00:41:29.380
for acoustic waves.

00:41:29.380 --> 00:41:33.300
So again, you can have the--

00:41:36.310 --> 00:41:38.590
if you look at those
relations, you can still

00:41:38.590 --> 00:41:41.560
have total internal reflection.

00:41:41.560 --> 00:41:45.380
Acoustic wave can also be
totally internally reflected.

00:41:45.380 --> 00:41:48.790
So if you go from
medium, like, say--

00:41:48.790 --> 00:41:51.490
I think it's germanium,
for example, to silicon.

00:41:51.490 --> 00:41:57.070
If you launch,
say, lattice wave,

00:41:57.070 --> 00:41:58.630
and you look at
the relation, there

00:41:58.630 --> 00:42:01.400
is a total critical angle.

00:42:01.400 --> 00:42:04.100
And above the critical
angle, it will reflect

00:42:04.100 --> 00:42:06.220
total internal reflection.

00:42:06.220 --> 00:42:10.690
And then on the other side, you
have correspondingly evanescent

00:42:10.690 --> 00:42:12.415
wave.

00:42:12.415 --> 00:42:15.370
It doesn't carry energy
to the other side.

00:42:15.370 --> 00:42:20.420
But at the time it got there
is vibration exponential decay

00:42:20.420 --> 00:42:23.270
from the surface.

00:42:23.270 --> 00:42:27.400
So all those waves
have similar phenomena.

00:42:27.400 --> 00:42:30.950
And so the equations you start
with could be very different.

00:42:30.950 --> 00:42:35.450
But general wave behavior
share a lot of similarities.

00:42:38.100 --> 00:42:39.420
OK.

00:42:39.420 --> 00:42:46.040
So that's waves at
a single interface.

00:42:46.040 --> 00:42:48.500
And what we're
moving on there is,

00:42:48.500 --> 00:42:53.470
what about multiple interfaces?

00:42:53.470 --> 00:42:57.770
And single interface is only
reflection, refraction--

00:42:57.770 --> 00:42:59.415
I do want-- before
I reach this, I

00:42:59.415 --> 00:43:01.990
do want to make another comment.

00:43:01.990 --> 00:43:04.360
We said that, OK, because
this is Snell's law, if you

00:43:04.360 --> 00:43:09.460
put any of the negative,
you draw your light, how

00:43:09.460 --> 00:43:14.170
the refraction should happen,
then if n is negative,

00:43:14.170 --> 00:43:15.400
it goes this way.

00:43:18.200 --> 00:43:20.160
Your theta goes the other way.

00:43:20.160 --> 00:43:22.460
So that's why at
the beginning, there

00:43:22.460 --> 00:43:24.830
are a lot of news
articles of light bends

00:43:24.830 --> 00:43:28.010
the wrong way, all this
stuff, with electromagnetic

00:43:28.010 --> 00:43:31.150
metamaterials.

00:43:31.150 --> 00:43:32.590
OK.

00:43:32.590 --> 00:43:39.565
So what we are approaching next
is multi layers, thin films.

00:44:01.420 --> 00:44:03.130
In thin films.

00:44:03.130 --> 00:44:08.490
So when I-- I said that
before we have one interface.

00:44:08.490 --> 00:44:10.450
Now let's go to two interfaces.

00:44:10.450 --> 00:44:14.270
And so to go from two
interfaces to any interface

00:44:14.270 --> 00:44:18.170
is actually quite
straightforward.

00:44:18.170 --> 00:44:21.920
And so the physics,
what happens is

00:44:21.920 --> 00:44:26.390
I have the wave comes
in the first interface.

00:44:26.390 --> 00:44:30.530
And let's say n1, n2, n3.

00:44:30.530 --> 00:44:33.830
My formula is written
in terms of small n,

00:44:33.830 --> 00:44:39.920
but you can write it capital
N. Everything is identical.

00:44:39.920 --> 00:44:43.490
And so what happens is
that the first interface,

00:44:43.490 --> 00:44:47.640
you have refraction,
you have reflection.

00:44:47.640 --> 00:44:52.710
You can use your Fresnel formula
to calculate the reflection

00:44:52.710 --> 00:44:55.890
and refraction, transmit it.

00:44:55.890 --> 00:44:59.070
And when it comes to the
second, you propagate.

00:44:59.070 --> 00:45:02.815
We know how to write the wave
propagation, the phase factor

00:45:02.815 --> 00:45:03.315
here.

00:45:05.900 --> 00:45:07.070
We have the phase factor.

00:45:12.280 --> 00:45:14.130
That's my phase factor.

00:45:14.130 --> 00:45:18.450
So when the z charges, I
know how to write it up

00:45:18.450 --> 00:45:20.420
for different media.

00:45:20.420 --> 00:45:24.050
And then at the second
interface, you have reflection,

00:45:24.050 --> 00:45:27.190
you have transmission.

00:45:27.190 --> 00:45:29.020
And once it's
transmitting into n3,

00:45:29.020 --> 00:45:30.970
I don't have
anything coming back,

00:45:30.970 --> 00:45:34.810
so I can say that
part is going forward.

00:45:34.810 --> 00:45:37.120
This one coming
back is troublesome,

00:45:37.120 --> 00:45:41.480
because it comes back
into the interface again.

00:45:41.480 --> 00:45:46.310
And then I get, again,
transmission from 2 to 1.

00:45:46.310 --> 00:45:48.770
And reflection,
every time I can use

00:45:48.770 --> 00:45:54.740
my Fresnel formula plus the
propagating phase factor

00:45:54.740 --> 00:45:56.340
to trace it.

00:45:56.340 --> 00:46:02.220
So transmission, reflection.

00:46:02.220 --> 00:46:03.660
And that's one way to do it.

00:46:03.660 --> 00:46:09.240
You add up the field, you
use the Fresnel formula.

00:46:09.240 --> 00:46:12.120
But it's pretty tedious.

00:46:12.120 --> 00:46:15.135
I can say-- you can say it's an
infinite series, because there

00:46:15.135 --> 00:46:18.930
are infinite number of
reflection and transmission

00:46:18.930 --> 00:46:21.330
in theory.

00:46:21.330 --> 00:46:27.640
So that's-- you trace the field.

00:46:27.640 --> 00:46:35.230
And if you don't consider the
phase, of the light in EM wave,

00:46:35.230 --> 00:46:38.360
then that's-- people do--
this is pretty much like Monte

00:46:38.360 --> 00:46:38.860
Carlo.

00:46:38.860 --> 00:46:40.360
People do ray tracing.

00:46:40.360 --> 00:46:43.612
They trace the trajectory
of light bundles.

00:46:43.612 --> 00:46:45.070
And there are
commercial code to do

00:46:45.070 --> 00:46:49.020
that in complicated geometry.

00:46:49.020 --> 00:46:53.400
But when you have those
phase also changing,

00:46:53.400 --> 00:46:57.330
it becomes a lot more
complicated to actually trace

00:46:57.330 --> 00:46:59.940
and carry your
phase of the field,

00:46:59.940 --> 00:47:04.850
and not very
computationally efficient.

00:47:04.850 --> 00:47:08.620
So at least for thin films,
flat structures, there

00:47:08.620 --> 00:47:09.820
different way--

00:47:09.820 --> 00:47:11.240
other ways to do it.

00:47:11.240 --> 00:47:16.540
And the next way is
here, at each interface,

00:47:16.540 --> 00:47:20.860
I can use the Fresnel formula.

00:47:20.860 --> 00:47:22.960
But see, the last one is--

00:47:22.960 --> 00:47:26.410
I don't care about the--

00:47:26.410 --> 00:47:28.900
I add this all together,
because the phase vector

00:47:28.900 --> 00:47:31.490
is same for each one.

00:47:31.490 --> 00:47:33.860
So OK, I say, this
is my incidence,

00:47:33.860 --> 00:47:36.680
this is a total
resulting reflection.

00:47:39.520 --> 00:47:43.000
And this is my total
forward propagating

00:47:43.000 --> 00:47:47.900
and my reflected
propagating inside the film.

00:47:47.900 --> 00:47:52.370
And this is my
total transmittance.

00:47:52.370 --> 00:47:55.830
And in that case, I do not know
the relation between this point

00:47:55.830 --> 00:47:58.500
at this point, because
this point is summing up

00:47:58.500 --> 00:48:00.630
many of those forward.

00:48:00.630 --> 00:48:03.930
Same, this is a summing
up many backward.

00:48:03.930 --> 00:48:06.180
So I have to use my
boundary condition

00:48:06.180 --> 00:48:13.940
to refine the relations among
the incident, reflected.

00:48:13.940 --> 00:48:16.910
Here is forward,
backward, transmitted.

00:48:16.910 --> 00:48:20.000
So this is another way to do
it using boundary condition.

00:48:22.680 --> 00:48:25.770
And this is a resulting wave.

00:48:25.770 --> 00:48:28.380
Let's say positive,
this is a backward

00:48:28.380 --> 00:48:33.900
propagating reflective incident.

00:48:33.900 --> 00:48:36.990
And of course, there are
different polarization.

00:48:36.990 --> 00:48:39.210
If I consider TM
wave, then that's

00:48:39.210 --> 00:48:43.030
what I have to do as we
do a single in plane.

00:48:43.030 --> 00:48:47.060
And here is transmitted.

00:48:47.060 --> 00:48:50.965
So that's a way that I'm going
to follow through the math.

00:48:53.780 --> 00:48:56.060
And even this way--

00:48:56.060 --> 00:49:00.290
this way is pretty
intuitive mathematically.

00:49:00.290 --> 00:49:02.390
But even this way is
not very convenient

00:49:02.390 --> 00:49:05.710
if you have many layers.

00:49:05.710 --> 00:49:10.260
And if you have many layers, the
best is using a matrix format.

00:49:13.600 --> 00:49:17.580
And the matrix format is not--

00:49:17.580 --> 00:49:20.650
I consider total electric field.

00:49:20.650 --> 00:49:23.440
This is incident
reflective, right?

00:49:23.440 --> 00:49:25.480
But I don't distinguish
the incident reflective.

00:49:25.480 --> 00:49:30.950
The total local electric field
and the total magnetic field--

00:49:30.950 --> 00:49:33.650
so here I have--

00:49:33.650 --> 00:49:40.355
and the point I want to
use is the continuity.

00:49:40.355 --> 00:49:43.220
If you recall, the boundary
condition for EM wave

00:49:43.220 --> 00:49:48.680
is at the interface, I have
the tangential component

00:49:48.680 --> 00:49:50.900
of electric field,
the magnetic field,

00:49:50.900 --> 00:49:54.440
as long as there's no
sheet current there,

00:49:54.440 --> 00:49:58.620
is equal on both sides.

00:49:58.620 --> 00:50:03.510
So what I'm going
to do is I use--

00:50:03.510 --> 00:50:07.410
I know the phase factor from
this point to this point

00:50:07.410 --> 00:50:09.310
and from this point
to this point.

00:50:09.310 --> 00:50:12.570
So I know, actually, the
relation-- the phase--

00:50:12.570 --> 00:50:15.690
the relation between the
electric and magnetic field

00:50:15.690 --> 00:50:17.340
between these two points.

00:50:17.340 --> 00:50:20.680
And these two points
will be reflected as--

00:50:20.680 --> 00:50:26.980
let's say I have Ex 0 Hy 0--

00:50:26.980 --> 00:50:30.090
that's at this point--

00:50:30.090 --> 00:50:32.250
relate to this point
through a matrix.

00:50:35.420 --> 00:50:38.140
And that matrix, you can
get from the solution

00:50:38.140 --> 00:50:39.850
of Maxwell equation.

00:50:39.850 --> 00:50:45.400
And then to this point is Ex--

00:50:45.400 --> 00:50:49.060
let's say here I have
d is the thickness--

00:50:51.790 --> 00:50:56.350
and Hy d.

00:50:56.350 --> 00:51:03.280
So this matrix will make
my life much easier,

00:51:03.280 --> 00:51:07.630
because if I know the-- so
you relate the electric field

00:51:07.630 --> 00:51:08.590
total--

00:51:08.590 --> 00:51:10.720
it's not forward backward.

00:51:10.720 --> 00:51:13.890
It's added up all
forward and backward,

00:51:13.890 --> 00:51:16.710
total electric field--

00:51:16.710 --> 00:51:20.100
total magnetic
field at this point

00:51:20.100 --> 00:51:21.900
through the-- total
electric field,

00:51:21.900 --> 00:51:25.170
total magnetic field
through a matrix element.

00:51:25.170 --> 00:51:29.760
And I know my continuity is
propagating to the other side.

00:51:29.760 --> 00:51:31.650
This electric field
and magnetic field

00:51:31.650 --> 00:51:34.830
is the same on both sides.

00:51:34.830 --> 00:51:37.890
So this point is the
same as this point.

00:51:37.890 --> 00:51:40.760
So I can connect the many,
many layers together just

00:51:40.760 --> 00:51:44.420
by matrix
multiplication, continue

00:51:44.420 --> 00:51:47.650
this to build up any layer.

00:51:47.650 --> 00:51:50.850
So this one is most
convenient to use.

00:51:50.850 --> 00:51:52.050
And that's transfer matrix.

00:51:59.950 --> 00:52:05.230
And in fact, the transfer
matrix is used widely

00:52:05.230 --> 00:52:08.040
for many different problems.

00:52:08.040 --> 00:52:10.590
There are people dealing
with the multi-layer heat

00:52:10.590 --> 00:52:12.870
conduction.

00:52:12.870 --> 00:52:14.850
And in that case,
your continuity

00:52:14.850 --> 00:52:20.320
is temperature and heat flux at
the interface and continuous.

00:52:20.320 --> 00:52:23.760
So using that, if you can
relate the temperature and heat

00:52:23.760 --> 00:52:27.510
flux at this point to this
point and across the interface

00:52:27.510 --> 00:52:30.810
continues, so you just
write a similar matrix,

00:52:30.810 --> 00:52:35.090
and you do your matrix
multiplication for many layers.

00:52:35.090 --> 00:52:38.900
So it's a very useful
mathematical approach

00:52:38.900 --> 00:52:42.320
for many different problems.

00:52:42.320 --> 00:52:46.580
But what I'm going to do is
spend a little time here.

00:52:46.580 --> 00:52:51.110
And let's use this to
get the basic idea how

00:52:51.110 --> 00:52:54.900
I'm going to do multi layers.

00:52:54.900 --> 00:53:08.740
And what we need to
write down is essentially

00:53:08.740 --> 00:53:12.850
like two-interface condition.

00:53:12.850 --> 00:53:15.850
Before last lecture,
we wrote one interface,

00:53:15.850 --> 00:53:17.500
we had two equations.

00:53:17.500 --> 00:53:21.730
And now I have two
interfaces, and I

00:53:21.730 --> 00:53:23.980
need to write a propagating--

00:53:23.980 --> 00:53:25.450
the phase I need to carry.

00:53:25.450 --> 00:53:27.340
Because from this
point to this point,

00:53:27.340 --> 00:53:29.110
there's a distance traveled.

00:53:29.110 --> 00:53:37.770
And if I look at this plane
wave when go z is moving.

00:53:37.770 --> 00:53:42.030
Omega doesn't-- is the
same, and the x component,

00:53:42.030 --> 00:53:45.210
that's what is the
Snell's law take care.

00:53:45.210 --> 00:53:47.280
So this one, I don't--

00:53:47.280 --> 00:53:49.190
I don't need to worry.

00:53:49.190 --> 00:53:52.590
All Snell's law is always valid.

00:53:52.590 --> 00:53:54.720
And omega t, I
don't need to worry.

00:53:54.720 --> 00:53:58.180
What I need to worry is
carrying this phase term,

00:53:58.180 --> 00:54:00.670
this z term, because I go from--

00:54:00.670 --> 00:54:03.860
this is my z direction.

00:54:03.860 --> 00:54:08.390
When I go from this point
to this point, I have--

00:54:08.390 --> 00:54:10.220
this is the original 0 to d.

00:54:10.220 --> 00:54:13.040
That's my z.

00:54:13.040 --> 00:54:14.180
OK.

00:54:14.180 --> 00:54:25.740
So what I have, you can
write your Ei, the incident,

00:54:25.740 --> 00:54:31.950
and the exponential minus
i, the phase factor,

00:54:31.950 --> 00:54:34.305
I write the z direction n2--

00:54:37.750 --> 00:54:49.040
no, see, n1-- n1 cosine
theta i and lambda z.

00:54:49.040 --> 00:54:52.430
So this is my incident.

00:54:52.430 --> 00:54:57.590
And similarly,
reflected-- so you

00:54:57.590 --> 00:55:01.760
have to be comfortable
in carrying

00:55:01.760 --> 00:55:06.410
this positive n1 cosine theta i.

00:55:06.410 --> 00:55:08.870
Because of the
Snell's law, so theta,

00:55:08.870 --> 00:55:12.800
the angle is the same, z.

00:55:12.800 --> 00:55:16.790
So this is the incident
reflective in medium 1.

00:55:16.790 --> 00:55:23.197
And same, you can write the
transmitted, before plug

00:55:23.197 --> 00:55:25.790
in the E plus.

00:55:25.790 --> 00:55:29.000
Like I said, I do not--
this is not just--

00:55:29.000 --> 00:55:30.860
the origin is not just from Ei.

00:55:30.860 --> 00:55:32.880
It's from the model reflection.

00:55:32.880 --> 00:55:34.650
So I don't know the relation.

00:55:34.650 --> 00:55:42.860
So I want to say this is E,
let's say, plus exponential.

00:55:42.860 --> 00:55:49.110
Phase factor inside
is i n2 cosine theta--

00:55:49.110 --> 00:55:53.000
let's say here I'm using 2--

00:55:53.000 --> 00:55:56.720
and lambda z.

00:55:56.720 --> 00:56:04.870
And E minus exponential, that
is the opposite direction,

00:56:04.870 --> 00:56:10.900
n2 cosine theta 2 lambda z.

00:56:10.900 --> 00:56:14.120
You can also write the
transmission there.

00:56:14.120 --> 00:56:19.060
Now I apply the continuity,
the same boundary condition

00:56:19.060 --> 00:56:21.820
as I did before, for
the first interface

00:56:21.820 --> 00:56:23.920
and for the second interface.

00:56:23.920 --> 00:56:27.700
The tangential component of
the electric and magnetic field

00:56:27.700 --> 00:56:30.570
are continuous.

00:56:30.570 --> 00:56:35.230
So for the first interface,
when I take my original of z

00:56:35.230 --> 00:56:41.670
equals 0, cleverly
choosing your coordinate,

00:56:41.670 --> 00:56:47.130
so that is the
tangential component,

00:56:47.130 --> 00:56:59.730
so Ei times cosine theta i
plus Er cosine theta r equals

00:56:59.730 --> 00:57:08.130
E plus cosine theta 2 plus
E minus cosine theta 2.

00:57:10.950 --> 00:57:15.770
So that's the tangential
component on then two

00:57:15.770 --> 00:57:19.070
sides, this side and this side.

00:57:19.070 --> 00:57:22.490
Remember, I always
add up the total.

00:57:22.490 --> 00:57:24.790
I didn't say it's the incident.

00:57:24.790 --> 00:57:26.270
It's the total electric field.

00:57:26.270 --> 00:57:26.780
Yes?

00:57:26.780 --> 00:57:29.495
AUDIENCE: Is the theta
r from the [INAUDIBLE]??

00:57:29.495 --> 00:57:30.620
GANG CHEN: Yeah, I'm sorry.

00:57:30.620 --> 00:57:32.390
So this one is the same.

00:57:32.390 --> 00:57:35.250
Theta i and theta r
is the same, theta i.

00:57:35.250 --> 00:57:36.290
So the Snell's law.

00:57:39.440 --> 00:57:43.250
And then the
magnetic field is n--

00:57:43.250 --> 00:57:54.610
remember, it's n1 mu
C0 and Ei minus Er.

00:57:54.610 --> 00:58:00.070
That's the relation with
the Hy we derived last time.

00:58:00.070 --> 00:58:01.840
The relation-- if I
know electric field,

00:58:01.840 --> 00:58:02.980
I know the magnetic field.

00:58:02.980 --> 00:58:06.160
It's n mu C times
the electric field.

00:58:06.160 --> 00:58:09.670
For opposite propagating,
because of the direction

00:58:09.670 --> 00:58:10.240
change.

00:58:10.240 --> 00:58:15.430
My right-handed rule, I have
a negative sign in the front.

00:58:15.430 --> 00:58:27.280
And the same is n2 mu C0
E plus and minus E minus.

00:58:27.280 --> 00:58:30.970
So this is the
amplitude, E plus E

00:58:30.970 --> 00:58:34.510
minus, of the forward
propagating and backward

00:58:34.510 --> 00:58:37.090
propagating waves.

00:58:37.090 --> 00:58:39.670
And of course, I need
two other equations,

00:58:39.670 --> 00:58:44.530
because I have E plus, E
minus unknown, Er unknown,

00:58:44.530 --> 00:58:46.090
and also transmitted unknown.

00:58:46.090 --> 00:58:49.450
I need four more-- two more
equations to close this.

00:58:49.450 --> 00:58:51.445
That's at the other interface.

00:58:51.445 --> 00:58:52.480
I have two interfaces.

00:58:52.480 --> 00:58:55.000
Each interface gives
me two equations.

00:58:57.880 --> 00:59:01.780
So the other
interface, now you have

00:59:01.780 --> 00:59:06.880
to carry your phase
factor, because when

00:59:06.880 --> 00:59:17.850
the wave propagating from 0
to d, so you have to write z

00:59:17.850 --> 00:59:20.720
equals d now.

00:59:20.720 --> 00:59:26.800
Again, your electric field
is the E plus here projecting

00:59:26.800 --> 00:59:28.160
into the x direction.

00:59:28.160 --> 00:59:30.470
This is my x direction.

00:59:30.470 --> 00:59:33.000
It's cosine theta.

00:59:33.000 --> 00:59:39.320
So it's cosine theta
2 exponential i phi--

00:59:39.320 --> 00:59:40.850
minus i phi 2.

00:59:40.850 --> 00:59:43.580
Phi 2 is my phase factor.

00:59:43.580 --> 00:59:47.690
That's to say you can look
at that as n2 cosine theta 2.

00:59:47.690 --> 00:59:51.160
So let me write it down.

00:59:51.160 --> 01:00:00.130
So phi 2 equals n2 cosine
theta 2 over lambda times d.

01:00:00.130 --> 01:00:11.340
So E minus i phi 2 plus E minus
and cosine theta 2 E i phi 2--

01:00:11.340 --> 01:00:14.550
this is a backward, going back.

01:00:14.550 --> 01:00:18.480
So the z wave vector
is going backwards,

01:00:18.480 --> 01:00:21.420
so that's my plus
wave, plus sign.

01:00:25.400 --> 01:00:28.610
And then say my Et transmitted.

01:00:28.610 --> 01:00:37.185
So Et cosine theta t
exponential minus i phi 3.

01:00:40.480 --> 01:00:47.305
And this phi 3 is you do
n3 cosine theta 3 lambda

01:00:47.305 --> 01:00:48.850
all times d.

01:00:48.850 --> 01:00:50.450
It doesn't matter.

01:00:50.450 --> 01:00:54.010
In fact, you can restart your
coordinates, this phase factor

01:00:54.010 --> 01:00:57.805
at the end, because when you
calculate the transmitted,

01:00:57.805 --> 01:00:59.920
this cancels or
drops out this phase

01:00:59.920 --> 01:01:03.660
factor when you do the ratio.

01:01:03.660 --> 01:01:09.810
And so I have the
magnetic field is n2 mu

01:01:09.810 --> 01:01:15.840
C0 E plus minus E minus.

01:01:15.840 --> 01:01:26.045
And this is n3 mu C0 and Et
exponential minus i phi 3.

01:01:29.060 --> 01:01:34.442
Now I have four equations,
I can solve for the--

01:01:34.442 --> 01:01:38.570
what I want is really the
reflecting to incident--

01:01:38.570 --> 01:01:40.250
that's reflectance--

01:01:40.250 --> 01:01:45.180
reflection coefficient,
transmitted to incident--

01:01:45.180 --> 01:01:49.990
that's the transmission
coefficient.

01:01:49.990 --> 01:01:53.390
So I have enough equations
to solve that problem.

01:01:53.390 --> 01:02:05.320
And what you find is your r
equals Er over Ei equals r12--

01:02:05.320 --> 01:02:09.340
that's the Fresnel coefficient
between 1 and 2 interface--

01:02:09.340 --> 01:02:13.010
plus r23-- that's
Fresnel coefficient--

01:02:13.010 --> 01:02:20.460
exponential 2 i phi
2 and divided by 1

01:02:20.460 --> 01:02:28.760
plus r12 r23
exponential 2 i phi 2.

01:02:28.760 --> 01:02:32.480
So this is the
reflection coefficient.

01:02:32.480 --> 01:02:36.930
And similarly, you can do
your transmission coefficient.

01:02:36.930 --> 01:02:48.020
The Et Ei equals t12
t23 exponential i phi 2,

01:02:48.020 --> 01:02:57.350
and 1 plus r12 r23
exponential 2 i phi 2.

01:02:57.350 --> 01:02:59.600
So some comments here.

01:02:59.600 --> 01:03:03.660
So you can see the transmission,
you have E i phi 2.

01:03:03.660 --> 01:03:04.950
That's the first pass.

01:03:04.950 --> 01:03:08.520
So it doesn't have double there.

01:03:08.520 --> 01:03:13.020
And the denominator, reflecting
that it's always double,

01:03:13.020 --> 01:03:18.900
so you have 2 i phi 2,
double the back and forth.

01:03:18.900 --> 01:03:20.820
If you look at this,
the next transmission

01:03:20.820 --> 01:03:24.180
is reflecting, come
back, and go out.

01:03:24.180 --> 01:03:26.190
Reflection is the first one.

01:03:26.190 --> 01:03:31.960
And then the next one is
have to go forward, reflect,

01:03:31.960 --> 01:03:33.850
go back, go out.

01:03:33.850 --> 01:03:39.250
So that's where this
factor of 2 comes from.

01:03:39.250 --> 01:03:42.220
And second, you will say
that if you square this,

01:03:42.220 --> 01:03:45.000
you'll get a sine
cosine, so you get

01:03:45.000 --> 01:03:49.450
an interference, periodical
variations of reflectance

01:03:49.450 --> 01:03:53.890
and transmittance as a
function of this phase factor.

01:03:53.890 --> 01:03:55.570
So that's the
interference effect.

01:03:58.510 --> 01:04:00.790
OK.

01:04:00.790 --> 01:04:05.540
And I do want to
get the math done.

01:04:05.540 --> 01:04:09.040
So let me first
give you the math

01:04:09.040 --> 01:04:12.800
and then do the discussion
in the next lecture.

01:04:12.800 --> 01:04:19.280
So I have gone through the
result and the wave method

01:04:19.280 --> 01:04:22.160
and really, say, split
the electric field

01:04:22.160 --> 01:04:24.680
into forward and backward.

01:04:24.680 --> 01:04:28.550
But I said that if I do this,
it's still not very convenient.

01:04:28.550 --> 01:04:30.380
And the best thing
is to do this--

01:04:30.380 --> 01:04:33.070
transfer matrix.

01:04:33.070 --> 01:04:36.490
And using the fact that the
both electric and magnetic field

01:04:36.490 --> 01:04:40.870
are continuous at the interface,
so what I need is to write.

01:04:40.870 --> 01:04:44.950
So here, I'm going to ask you
to read more detail in the book.

01:04:52.840 --> 01:04:57.210
And if I do, I want to
get the transfer matrix.

01:04:57.210 --> 01:04:59.880
But eventually, I
have to also come back

01:04:59.880 --> 01:05:04.230
to what I care is the ratio.

01:05:04.230 --> 01:05:07.410
So I care about reflecting
the electric field divided

01:05:07.410 --> 01:05:08.040
by incident.

01:05:08.040 --> 01:05:08.760
So I have--

01:05:08.760 --> 01:05:12.340
I said what I do in
math is the total.

01:05:12.340 --> 01:05:15.300
Total electric
field is continuous,

01:05:15.300 --> 01:05:17.130
total magnetic is continuous.

01:05:17.130 --> 01:05:21.420
But at the end, I really want
to still have to separate them

01:05:21.420 --> 01:05:23.340
for incident and reflection.

01:05:23.340 --> 01:05:26.260
So I need a matrix
to relate that.

01:05:26.260 --> 01:05:32.350
And so if you do
your electric field--

01:05:32.350 --> 01:05:35.430
so this is at 0.

01:05:35.430 --> 01:05:39.780
This is my z, and this is my x.

01:05:39.780 --> 01:05:45.310
And let's say this
is a 1, this is a 2.

01:05:45.310 --> 01:05:54.090
And so 1, x component
0, is Eix plus the Erx.

01:05:57.080 --> 01:06:05.630
And H1y at 0, so I'm
considering a TM wave.

01:06:05.630 --> 01:06:18.260
So H1y is n1 mu C0
and Ei minus Ex.

01:06:18.260 --> 01:06:22.190
You can see now I have a
relation between the incident

01:06:22.190 --> 01:06:27.110
reflected electric
field to total incident

01:06:27.110 --> 01:06:32.420
electric field, total
incident magnetic field.

01:06:32.420 --> 01:06:34.230
I can inverse this.

01:06:34.230 --> 01:06:36.820
And so I write into matrix.

01:06:36.820 --> 01:06:39.150
So this one, essentially
what-- because I

01:06:39.150 --> 01:06:47.220
want to write everything to
matrix, H0, H1, 1, and here

01:06:47.220 --> 01:06:50.070
is 0.

01:06:50.070 --> 01:07:04.140
And this is 1, 1, 1 over p1,
minus 1 over p1, and Eix, Erx.

01:07:04.140 --> 01:07:07.050
So I have a--

01:07:07.050 --> 01:07:11.020
this p1 is the
surface impedance.

01:07:11.020 --> 01:07:14.010
So it's Ex Hy.

01:07:14.010 --> 01:07:24.420
And for the TM wave, we have
this cosine theta and n mu C0.

01:07:24.420 --> 01:07:27.450
So this is for TM wave.

01:07:27.450 --> 01:07:32.310
And TE wave, you can write
a different expression.

01:07:32.310 --> 01:07:41.670
So what I wrote down here is
the total electrical field,

01:07:41.670 --> 01:07:44.940
total magnetic field
related to incident

01:07:44.940 --> 01:07:48.435
reflected electric
field through matrix.

01:07:51.400 --> 01:07:54.910
So that's either the first
side, because eventually I

01:07:54.910 --> 01:08:00.530
want to find the Ei/Er relation,
not just the H/E relation.

01:08:00.530 --> 01:08:04.890
But see, what I'm using is
E, and the H is continuous.

01:08:04.890 --> 01:08:08.040
So E and H--

01:08:08.040 --> 01:08:19.630
so E1x 0, E1y 0 should
equal E2x 0, H2y 0.

01:08:19.630 --> 01:08:25.120
So that's the continuity
of tangential component.

01:08:25.120 --> 01:08:28.210
So the second is my--

01:08:28.210 --> 01:08:33.100
if you do your-- if you go back
to what we just written down

01:08:33.100 --> 01:08:37.359
on the electric field and
relate to magnetic field,

01:08:37.359 --> 01:08:43.220
you'll find out is H2--

01:08:43.220 --> 01:08:43.720
no.

01:08:47.120 --> 01:08:57.260
E2x 0 and the H2y 0, so I
want to relate this point

01:08:57.260 --> 01:08:58.250
to this point.

01:08:58.250 --> 01:08:59.540
That's in the same media.

01:08:59.540 --> 01:09:02.590
That's just the phase factor.

01:09:02.590 --> 01:09:06.770
So if I do that relation,
that gave me matrix.

01:09:06.770 --> 01:09:11.649
So this point is related
to the other point.

01:09:11.649 --> 01:09:21.530
What I want is E2x
at d, H2y at d.

01:09:21.530 --> 01:09:28.050
So this is this point here,
here, these two points.

01:09:28.050 --> 01:09:31.290
And in between is
this matrix I want.

01:09:31.290 --> 01:09:49.819
And that's cosine phi 2 minus
i p2 sine phi 2 and minus i

01:09:49.819 --> 01:09:57.200
p2 sine phi 2 and cosine phi 2.

01:09:57.200 --> 01:09:59.750
So this is my M,
transfer matrix.

01:09:59.750 --> 01:10:07.030
That's what I was
saying here, OK?

01:10:07.030 --> 01:10:14.460
And the beauty of this
is I relate this point

01:10:14.460 --> 01:10:15.900
to this point.

01:10:15.900 --> 01:10:21.570
Now, if I add another layer,
layer 3, between here and here

01:10:21.570 --> 01:10:23.730
is continuous.

01:10:23.730 --> 01:10:27.000
So this point is
also this point.

01:10:27.000 --> 01:10:30.750
Now, to find this point, I
tie my M's, the other M here.

01:10:30.750 --> 01:10:33.340
If this is M1, this is M2.

01:10:33.340 --> 01:10:38.250
I times another M, I will
relate to this point.

01:10:38.250 --> 01:10:43.850
So with the transfer
matrix, this is my M.

01:10:43.850 --> 01:10:50.450
And see, if I have N layers,
it's just M1 times M2 times MN.

01:10:50.450 --> 01:10:52.370
So I can do any layers easily.

01:10:55.590 --> 01:10:57.230
OK.

01:10:57.230 --> 01:11:02.780
And finally is my
transmitted, the last one.

01:11:02.780 --> 01:11:08.160
When it goes out, it does not
have anything coming back.

01:11:08.160 --> 01:11:11.190
So I still need
to relate to that.

01:11:11.190 --> 01:11:25.510
So my final relation is--

01:11:25.510 --> 01:11:32.260
let's say if this is
transmitted, Ex d Hy d.

01:11:32.260 --> 01:11:45.110
And it equals 1, 1 over p3
Etx, because in this case,

01:11:45.110 --> 01:11:50.410
this is my 1, 2, 3, and
the transmitted field

01:11:50.410 --> 01:11:53.950
only has one forward
propagating, not backward.

01:11:53.950 --> 01:11:57.160
So I have a simple--

01:11:57.160 --> 01:12:02.000
with this, if you can see,
I can use all this relation.

01:12:02.000 --> 01:12:06.690
The first one-- because this one
is continuous equal this one,

01:12:06.690 --> 01:12:10.850
so I have, you can
see, this matrix--

01:12:10.850 --> 01:12:15.380
1, 1-- let me don't do this.

01:12:15.380 --> 01:12:19.600
I will use this one as my--

01:12:19.600 --> 01:12:23.490
this is my-- here is my A.

01:12:23.490 --> 01:12:27.860
Let's say this is
A. This is a matrix.

01:12:27.860 --> 01:12:37.940
So if I use my continuity as
A times Eix Hrx, that's here.

01:12:37.940 --> 01:12:39.800
That equals here.

01:12:39.800 --> 01:12:41.930
This equals this.

01:12:41.930 --> 01:12:43.920
That's equal to this.

01:12:43.920 --> 01:12:50.540
So equals M times
this one-- that's

01:12:50.540 --> 01:12:53.980
the other interface
that equals this.

01:12:53.980 --> 01:13:00.380
So 1, 1 p3, and Etx.

01:13:00.380 --> 01:13:04.100
So with this, I can
relate the transmitted

01:13:04.100 --> 01:13:09.270
to incident,
reflected to incident.

01:13:09.270 --> 01:13:11.880
So this is the--

01:13:11.880 --> 01:13:13.850
no matter how many
layers, I just

01:13:13.850 --> 01:13:15.890
add in the multiplication
of this matrix.

01:13:19.860 --> 01:13:23.890
And when you do--

01:13:23.890 --> 01:13:26.080
if do this kind of
calculation, this

01:13:26.080 --> 01:13:29.230
is where people
design any coating.

01:13:29.230 --> 01:13:32.110
The design coating they'll do
for-- as long as a thin film

01:13:32.110 --> 01:13:35.380
coating, you do a
transfer matrix,

01:13:35.380 --> 01:13:38.620
and it has various kind
of interference effects

01:13:38.620 --> 01:13:44.090
and come into desire--
the desirable value.

01:13:44.090 --> 01:13:46.540
And when you do those
kind of operations,

01:13:46.540 --> 01:13:49.840
the key is you
have to be careful,

01:13:49.840 --> 01:13:54.190
because sometimes this blew up.

01:13:54.190 --> 01:14:05.130
And the-- depends on whether,
for example, if your phi itself

01:14:05.130 --> 01:14:08.700
